/* panelfw.js - the panel firmware profiles: what one refresh of a scale looks
 * like under each board firmware, for the refresh simulation and the palette.
 *
 * ONE source for all three readers (2026-11-19): the designers' simulator
 * loads this file as a script (designer.html, inlined by build_designer.py);
 * conductor/panelfw.py reads the two JSON blocks between the markers below
 * and the Conductor sends them to its page in /api/state ("panel_fw"), next
 * to the palette, and conductor/export3d.py uses them for the 3D export. So
 * both blocks must stay strict JSON (double quotes, no comments, no trailing
 * commas) - tests/test_panelfw.py parses them.
 *
 * Nothing here reaches the units: the boards play whatever firmware they
 * carry. This only decides what the two screens SHOW a refresh as.
 *
 * THE FIRST BLOCK is edited by hand:
 *   timeline[code] = [shake_end, drive_start, drive_end] in waveform ticks
 *     from the start of that scale's refresh, read off each image's waveform
 *     table (tools/fw_waveform.py dump). Every colour's waveform is
 *     wave_ticks long (662) in both images and starts with lead_in_ticks
 *     (122) of lead-in; then the shake, a rest, and the colour drive,
 *     reaching the target at drive_end.
 *     FW_260923 (production): every colour finishes at its own time. Codes
 *       10, 12 and 15 end with a long drive block to 602, a rest, and a 1-2
 *       tick pulse at 658-660 / 658-659: drive_end is that last pulse, as
 *       the video shows for code 15 (still yellow until the pulse flips it
 *       to smoke blue - PM, 2026-11-19).
 *     FW_261119F (pinks): each colour's trailing rest moved to just after
 *       the shake, so every colour finishes within ticks 651..662.
 *   tick_s: 11.0 ms, the tick the reference video measured on the real
 *     panel (the firmware's nominal tick is 10 ms), so 662 ticks = 7.28 s.
 *   palette: the codes whose colour changes with the firmware. FW_261119F's
 *     0x0A pink purple No.13 and 0x0C pink red No.13 are taken from the
 *     photos in the colour chart spreadsheet, exposure-normalised - not yet
 *     measured on the panel, so both screens mark them (estimate: true).
 *     Every other code keeps conductor/look.py's PALETTE, the FW_260923
 *     chart. CHANGE THE PINKS HERE, then run tools/make_panelfw.py.
 *   recorded / synth: which measured curve each code plays (see below).
 *
 * THE SECOND BLOCK is written by tools/make_panelfw.py - do not edit it: per
 * profile and code, the colour the scale shows from each tick on
 * (keys: [[tick, "rrggbb"], ...], held until the next key) until `end`
 * (drive_end), where it shows the palette colour. Made from the reference
 * video of a refresh from white to each code on the real panel
 * (refresh_capture_planD.json beside this file, plan-D image = FW_261119F
 * with the two pinks exchanged - hence "recorded"), white-balanced so the
 * recorded white is the palette's white and each curve ends on its code's
 * palette colour. FW_260923 replays the same curves re-timed in ticks (its
 * drive starts right after the shake, the idle time moved to the end); its
 * 0x0A/0x0C (orange, olive grey) have no recording and are synthesised: the
 * lead-in and shake of "from", then the drive of "drive" (code 15, the same
 * tail shape) pulled towards their own colour, held from "hold" (602) and
 * switched to the palette colour at the final pulse.
 */
